WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:04.859
The reviews for Martin Scorsese's The Irishman
are in, and they're about as fantastic as

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:05.880
reviews can get.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:10.580
Based on Charles Brandt's 2004 book I Heard
You Paint Houses, The Irishman stars Robert

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:15.510
De Niro as former mafioso and alleged hitman
Frank "The Irishman" Sheeran, who details

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:18.780
the dirty deeds he did while working for the
Bufalino crime family.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:22.859
And there's particular focus on his involvement
in the disappearance and purported murder

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:27.080
of International Brotherhood of Teamsters
leader Jimmy Hoffa, played by Al Pacino.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:32.200
The Irishman had its world premiere at the
57th New York Film Festival on September 27th,

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.940
2019, when critics fell head over heels for
it.

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:40.460
As of September 30th, The Irishman stands
at a flawless 100 percent approval rating

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:41.400
on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:45.060
Writing for IndieWire, Eric Kohn said of the
legendary director's latest,

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:49.720
"It takes less than five minutes to establish
The Irishman in Martin Scorsese's unmistakable

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:54.460
voice [...] The mood is at once taut and funny,
the essence of Scorsese's ability to humanize

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:57.920
the mob as prickly macho men just a few notes
shy of lovable.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:01.600
In that fundamental disconnect — between
endearing people and the psychotic world they

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:05.900
represent — the movie presents a fascinating
onramp to America's obsession with organized

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:06.760
crime."

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:10.940
Kohn added that The Irishman is bursting with
"Scorsese's trademark style" and proves that

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:13.260
the director is, quote, "more alive than ever."

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:15.660
At The Movie Minute, Joanna Langfield wrote,

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:17.980
"Scorsese's mob epic demands and commands.

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:22.740
Confident enough to play with traditional
storytelling as well as cutting edge technology,

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:24.950
this is thrilling work from artists at their
prime.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:28.570
[...] It's a marvel to watch the piece as
a whole, with its shifting tones, laugh out

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:31.920
loud funny breaks, and its sneaky, very human
heartbeat."

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:36.410
J. Don Birnam at Splash Report says that there's
more to The Irishman than meets the eye, writing,

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:40.620
"As with everything Scorsese touches, The
Irishman is plenty more than just a criminal's

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:41.460
biography.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:46.000
Like many of his other films, it is a reflection
on human nature, on whether and how greed,

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.060
loyalty, betrayal, and regret, drive a life.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:54.040
It is also, more fundamentally, an aging director's
look back with nostalgia at his own work,

00:01:54.049 --> 00:01:58.010
at forgotten episodes in American history,
and about the meaning not just of life but

00:01:58.010 --> 00:01:59.290
of humanity itself."

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:04.720
Slashfilm's Chris Evangelista called The Irishman
a "funny, melancholy" masterpiece in his review,

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:05.520
writing,

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:06.740
"This is not Goodfellas.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:07.960
This is not Casino.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:13.300
This is Scorsese at his most reflective, crafting
a masterwork that finds the filmmaker reflecting

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:15.940
on everything he's done, and what it's all
amounted to.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:20.280
The results are breathtaking, and one of Martin
Scorsese's very best films."

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:24.480
The stellar cast of The Irishman received
high praise from critics, the vast majority

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.700
of whom felt each star turned in career-best
performances.

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:32.011
Vulture critic David Edelstein was impressed
by the major players in The Irishman, but

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:35.420
was completely floored by Joe Pesci as Russell
Bufalino.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:37.020
Edelstein wrote in his review,

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:41.090
"I heard all sorts of huzzahs about Pacino
— and he is wonderful — but it's Pesci

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:42.460
who thrilled me to the core."

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:43.900
"He likes to talk, don't he?"

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:49.930
Matt Zoller Seitz of RogerEbert.com wrote
that The Irishman solidifies De Niro as, quote,

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:53.709
"one of the great scene-stealing straight
men" in film history, while Vogue's Taylor

00:02:53.709 --> 00:02:57.280
Antrim said that "Pacino is just a scenery-chewing
riot as Hoffa."

00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:58.300
AV Club's A.A.

00:02:58.319 --> 00:03:03.099
Dowd commended both Scorsese's direction and
De Niro's enduring acting chops in writing

00:03:03.099 --> 00:03:07.120
that the filmmaker, quote, "brings out a subtle
agony from De Niro one might have assumed

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:08.860
the actor could no longer summon."

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:13.320
"What, you want to know if I did it or not?"

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.220
Stephanie Zacharek at TIME Magazine felt similarly,
writing,

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:22.000
"De Niro gives his best performance in years,
with zero mugging or scowling — his Frank

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:26.060
is a man of action who's so busy doing bad
stuff, he barely has time to think."

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:31.180
Some may be wary of The Irishman after hearing
that it clocks in at a staggering 209 minutes,

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:35.200
but most critics have promised that the hefty
runtime is part of what makes the film so

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:36.040
special.

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:38.060
As The New Yorker's Richard Brody wrote,

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:41.940
"It runs a minute shy of three and a half
hours, and I wouldn't wish it any shorter."

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:45.740
Brody certainly wasn't the only one who felt
this way about The Irishman's runtime.

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:46.980
At AV Club, A.A.

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:47.720
Dowd said,

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:53.080
"This is a remarkably brisk three-and-a-half
hours — Scorsese, at a ripe 76, still directs

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:57.239
with the energy of a hungry young filmmaker,
his command of montage yanking the audience

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:58.620
forward from scene to scene."

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:03.300
Over at Vox, Alissa Wilkinson argued that,
quote, "the near-bagginess of the film is

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:04.580
part of its initial charm."

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:05.360
She continued,

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:10.040
"The Irishman's long arc (which involves the
use of largely unobtrusive de-aging technology)

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:14.400
means the film follows Frank and his associates
long past when the movie usually ends, with

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:15.420
triumph or failure.

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:19.760
The film instead takes a distinct turn away
from rat-a-tat plotting and revenge toward

00:04:19.769 --> 00:04:22.210
a frankly stunning, contemplative movement."

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:26.720
Speaking of the digital de-aging in The Irishman
— used to make De Niro, Pesci, and Pacino

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:30.650
look decades younger — many critics agree
that while it can briefly take viewers out

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:33.380
of the film, the technology only adds to the
story.

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:34.880
Stephanie Zacharek wrote,

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:39.130
"The de-aging is distracting at first: The
actors' eyes look real, but their skin is

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:43.919
just a tad rubbery and flat — now and then
I had to stave off a PTSD flashback to Robert

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:45.540
Zemeckis' Polar Express.

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:49.580
But the special effects are hardly a deal
breaker, and in the end they probably add

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:51.240
to the movie's mythological vibe."

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:55.740
Mike Ryan at Uproxxx said that the de-aging
isn't perfect, but it's worth it, writing,

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:59.940
"This is the small price to pay to get all
these actors together again to tell this story."

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:04.140
Audiences can decide for themselves how they
feel about The Irishman when the film opens

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:08.760
in a limited capacity on November 1st, then
drops on Netflix on November 27th.

